{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-2-800-fall-2004", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["2.800"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Tribology", "course_slug": "2-800-tribology-fall-2004", "term": "Fall", "year": 2004, "as_taught_in": "Fall 2004", "language": "en", "departments": ["Mechanical Engineering"], "academic_levels": ["Graduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Nam Suh", "Dr. Nannaji Saka"], "subject_primary": "Materials Science and Engineering", "subject_secondary": ["Mechanical Engineering", "Mechanical Design", "Nanotechnology", "Science", "Physics", "Classical Mechanics"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Engineering", "subtopics": [{"name": "Materials Science and Engineering", "specialties": []}, {"name": "Mechanical Engineering", "specialties": ["Mechanical Design"]}, {"name": "Nanotechnology", "specialties": []}]}, {"name": "Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Physics", "specialties": ["Classical Mechanics"]}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Exams", "Lecture Notes", "Problem Sets", "Written Assignments"], "description": "This course addresses the design of tribological systems: the interfaces between two or more bodies in relative motion. Fundamental topics include: geometric, chemical, and physical characterization of surfaces; friction and wear mechanisms for metals, polymers, and ceramics, including abrasive wear, delamination theory, tool wear, erosive wear, wear of polymers and composites; and boundary lubrication and solid-film lubrication. The course also considers the relationship between nano-tribology and macro-tribology, rolling contacts, tribological problems in magnetic recording and electrical contacts, and monitoring and diagnosis of friction and wear. Case studies are used to illustrate key points.", "course_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-800-tribology-fall-2004/", "image_url": "http